\section{Head models and the MRI check}\label{sec:S4}

\subsection{The adult and the three classes of smaller head}\label{sec:S4.1}

The adult is the MNE sample subject \citep{gramfort2013}, with FreeSurfer surfaces and atlas labels \citep{desikan2006,fischl2012} and a three-layer BEM whose outer surface lies on the MRI scalp. Its usable cortex is << F.g3b_adult_cortex_area_cm2 >> cm². The smaller heads belong to three classes, which are not independent subjects (main text, Section 2.2).

The << F.wr_n_scaled_words >> scaled adults are copies of the adult scaled by one factor, so they differ from it in size only. The school-age size control is << F.g3b_cfg_school_scale >> times the adult, and the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_years >>-year size control has the head circumference of the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template. Both keep the adult's vertices, so $\Delta$ is taken vertex by vertex.

The << F.wr_n_templates_words >> infant templates at << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>, << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >> months \citep{oreilly2021} average many MRIs of the Neurodevelopmental MRI Database \citep{richards2016} and have smooth cortices. Their usable cortex is << F.g3b_infant2yr_cortex_area_cm2 >>, << F.g3b_infant18mo_cortex_area_cm2 >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_cortex_area_cm2 >> cm², << F.g3b_templates_cortex_area_pct_of_adult_range >> of the adult's, which lowers their total background power and their patch cancellation. They were used at their native size with their own three-layer BEMs, oct-6 source spaces, atlas labels and fiducials.

The << F.wr_n_children_words >> school-aged children A, B and C (<< F.g3b_children_age_range >> years; subjects << F.rev_qc_child_a_subject >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_subject >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_subject >>) are typically developing children of the OpenNeuro dataset ds005234 \citep{fadeev2024,fadeev2025}, which is de-identified and released under a CC0 license. Their own MRIs were processed by the dataset's authors. We used their white and sphere surfaces, atlas annotations and dense MRI scalp as distributed; these share each child's volume information. The dataset's boundary-element surfaces were also taken from the file tree as distributed, in which each subject's watershed surfaces sit in the preceding subject's folder. We matched these files to each child by the volume information they share with the child's own surfaces, and the storage objects behind them carry the name of the subject they belong to.

These watershed surfaces put the inner skull implausibly close to the scalp, a median << F.g3b_children_watershed_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >> mm below it over the upper head, with an outer skull crossing it. We therefore modeled the children's skull. The inner skull was moved inward to << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_depth >> mm below the scalp wherever the dataset's lay shallower, with no vertex within << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_min_cortex >> mm of a white-surface vertex. The surface between vertices comes within << F.g3b_children_white_to_inner_skull_closest_range >> mm of the white surface, and no white vertex lies outside it. The outer skull was placed about halfway between the inner skull and the scalp. Over the upper head, the modeled inner skull lies a median << F.g3b_children_modelled_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >> mm below the scalp. On the adult, whose segmented skull is known, the same procedure applied to a degraded inner skull changed the primary ratio by at most << F.g3b_bemval_max_ratio_change >> over the modeled skull depths. As for every head, the head surface of the forward model is the watershed outer skin placed on the MRI scalp.

The dataset provides no fiducials. The children's fiducials define the head frame and hence the centered placement, the origin of every scaled helmet and the coverage region of the OPM arrays. They were transferred from the adult by a similarity fit of the cortices and moved to the nearest scalp vertex. On the templates, which have their own fiducials, this transfer reproduced the fiducials to within << F.g3b_templates_fid_transfer_range >> mm and the head frames to within << F.g3b_templates_fid_transfer_rotation_range >>°. The largest error, << F.g3b_infant18mo_fid_transfer_nasion >> mm, was at the nasion of the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template, which lies farthest from the cortex.

Frame rotations of this size (total angles, their axis not resolved) are of the order of the rotations of the placement family (pitch ±<< F.g3b_cfg_pitch >>°, yaw ±<< F.g3b_cfg_yaw >>°, roll ±<< F.g3b_cfg_roll >>°; Section~\ref{sec:S5.1}). This comparison indicates rather than bounds their effect on the children's placements, and the transferred fiducials were not checked on the children's own MRIs. We did not use fiducials derived from Montreal Neurological Institute (MNI) coordinates: on the adult, such fiducials land << F.g3b_adult_mni_fid_range >> mm from the digitized ones.

Every head uses the adult's conductivities (<< F.meth_conductivity >> S/m). A single-compartment BEM (<< F.meth_bem1_conductivity >> S/m inside the inner skull) needs no skull or scalp conductivity. With it, the difference of medians from the adult was << F.g3b_smaller_bem1_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> dB, against << F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> dB with three layers. Across all smaller heads, that difference changed by << F.meth_bem1_change_db_range >> dB.

As distributed, the skull of the << F.meth_infant12mo_age_months >>-month template is << F.meth_infant12mo_skull_min_mm >> mm thick at its thinnest: << F.meth_infant12mo_thin_vertices >> of its << F.meth_infant12mo_inner_skull_vertices >> inner-skull vertices lie within << F.meth_infant12mo_thin_within_mm >> mm of the outer skull. The single-compartment BEM has no skull and is unaffected by this. With it, the template's $D$ was << F.meth_infant12mo_d_bem1_db >> dB, against << F.meth_infant12mo_d_db >> dB with three layers, and its difference from the adult was << F.meth_infant12mo_d_minus_adult_bem1_db >> dB, against << F.meth_infant12mo_d_minus_adult_db >> dB. The thin skull therefore does not carry the template's result.

\subsection{Regions}\label{sec:S4.2}

Regions are the lobes of the Desikan--Killiany atlas \citep{desikan2006}, grouped as follows. Frontal: superior, rostral middle and caudal middle frontal, pars opercularis, triangularis and orbitalis, lateral and medial orbitofrontal, precentral, paracentral and frontal pole. Parietal: superior and inferior parietal, supramarginal, postcentral and precuneus. Temporal: superior, middle and inferior temporal, banks of the superior temporal sulcus, fusiform, transverse temporal, entorhinal, temporal pole and parahippocampal. Occipital: lateral occipital, lingual, cuneus and pericalcarine. Cingulate: rostral and caudal anterior, posterior and isthmus. The sixth region is the insula. The medial wall belongs to no region, and both hemispheres are pooled.

We also report single parcels that the epilepsy literature emphasizes: precentral, superior temporal (``lateral temporal'' in Fig.~5 of the main text) and parahippocampal, with a mesial temporal group of the parahippocampal and entorhinal parcels; ``mesial temporal'' always means this group. All sources lie on the cortical surface, and the hippocampus and amygdala are not modeled. The parahippocampal and entorhinal cortex, << F.reg_mesial_parcels_depth_range >> mm below the scalp in the adult (median over targets), are therefore the nearest proxies for mesial temporal generators. The regions were chosen from the literature, and rankings among them are exploratory.

\subsection{The MRI quality check of the school-aged children and the infant templates}\label{sec:S4.3}

The children's white surfaces come within << F.g3b_children_white_to_scalp_closest_range >> mm of the scalp surface used for sensor placement and source depths. Child B has << F.g3b_child_b_shallow_targets_lt10mm >> cortical targets shallower than << F.g3b_shallow_target_limit_mm >> mm, and the adult has none. Child B's head is the size of the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template's (head circumference << F.g3b_child_b_ofc_cm >> against << F.g3b_infant2yr_ofc_cm >> cm; cap volume << F.g3b_child_b_cap_volume_cm3 >> against << F.g3b_infant2yr_cap_volume_cm3 >> cm³), while its cortex is much larger (<< F.g3b_child_b_cortex_area_cm2 >> against << F.g3b_infant2yr_cortex_area_cm2 >> cm²). Both observations pointed to a scalp surface lying inside the true scalp, or misregistered relative to the white surface. We therefore ran an MRI quality check on the three children, with the adult and the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template as references, using the surfaces exactly as the forward models use them. The same check was then run on the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates.

Along the outward normal of every vertex of the dense scalp, the T1 intensity was sampled from << F.rev_qc_profile_range_mm >> mm in << F.rev_qc_profile_step_mm >>-mm steps. The MRI head boundary is where this profile falls through the volume's air/tissue threshold (Otsu's method; the primary edge), with the half-maximum edge and the steepest gradient as secondary definitions. The signed offset of this boundary from the scalp used, positive outward, was summarized over the cap (the head above the plane of the fiducials) by its median, quartiles and 5th to 95th percentiles. It was also decomposed into a uniform offset, a translation and a rotation.

The frames were checked by comparing the volume geometry of every surface with the T1 header. The registration of the white surface to the T1 was checked by the white/gray contrast across it: the rigid shift that maximizes this contrast was searched coarsely and then finely, and we report the contrast gain of the best shift. The head circumference was measured both on the MRI head boundary and on the scalp used. Targets below << F.rev_qc_near_mm >> mm were counted against both surfaces.

A head is classed as misregistered if a surface's header differs from the T1 by more than the header tolerance, or if the white surface's realigning shift, edge translation or rotation displacement exceeds << F.rev_qc_tolerance_mm >> mm. It is classed as inside or outside if the cap median of the boundary's offset exceeds, or falls short of, the adult's by more than << F.rev_qc_tolerance_mm >> mm (one voxel) at both the Otsu and the half-maximum edge. Otherwise it is classed as usable.

We tested the check on the adult. A declared inward displacement of the adult's scalp by << F.rev_qc_test_inward_declared_mm >> mm was recovered as << F.rev_qc_test_inward_recovered_mm >> mm, and a << F.rev_qc_test_translation_declared_mm >>-mm translation as << F.rev_qc_test_translation_recovered_mm >> mm, with << F.rev_qc_test_translation_explained_pct >> of the offset pattern explained by a translation. A << F.rev_qc_test_white_shift_declared_mm >>-mm shift of the adult's white surface raised the T1 contrast at the surface by << F.rev_qc_test_white_contrast_gain_pct >> when the shift was undone, so a misregistered white surface would be found.

Every surface's header matched its T1, with corner displacements of at most << F.rev_qc_children_frame_max_disp_mm >> mm in the children (Fig.~\ref{fig:S6}). The children's white surfaces were registered: the best realigning shift was << F.rev_qc_children_white_best_shift_max_mm >> mm, the edge translation at most << F.rev_qc_children_white_translation_max_mm >> mm and the rotation displacement at most << F.rev_qc_children_white_rotation_disp_max_mm >> mm. The contrast gain was << F.rev_qc_child_a_white_contrast_gain_pct >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_white_contrast_gain_pct >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_white_contrast_gain_pct >> for children A, B and C.

In the children, the scalp used lay a median << F.wr_qc_children_offset_inside_otsu_mm_range >> mm inside the MRI head boundary at the Otsu edge (<< F.wr_qc_children_offset_inside_halfmax_mm_range >> mm at the half-maximum edge), against << F.wr_qc_adult_offset_inside_otsu_mm >> mm and << F.wr_qc_adult_offset_inside_halfmax_mm >> mm in the adult (Fig.~\ref{fig:S6}C). A FreeSurfer scalp thus sits just inside the T1 edge in every individual head, the adult's included. The scalp of the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template lay << F.wr_qc_infant2yr_offset_outside_otsu_mm >> mm outside its boundary (verdict: << F.rev_qc_infant2yr_verdict >>), which is consistent with an average template whose scalp is smoothed outward. The scalps of the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates lay << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_offset_outside_otsu_mm >> and << F.wr_qc_infant12mo_offset_outside_otsu_mm >> mm outside theirs (<< F.wr_qc_infant18mo_offset_outside_halfmax_mm >> and << F.wr_qc_infant12mo_offset_outside_halfmax_mm >> mm at the half-maximum edge, against << F.wr_qc_infant2yr_offset_outside_halfmax_mm >> mm for the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template).

The verdict for the << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month template was << F.wr_qc_infant12mo_verdict >>, and for the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_verdict >>. The white surface of the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template fitted the T1's white/gray contrast best after a rigid shift of << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_best_shift_mm >> mm, which raised the contrast by << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_contrast_gain_pct >>, against << F.wr_qc_templates_other_white_best_shift_mm >> mm in the other two templates. The T1 edge across it showed a translation of << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_edge_translation_mm >> mm; both values exceed the << F.rev_qc_tolerance_mm >>-mm tolerance. This is a fit over the whole surface, which single sections do not show; the template's white-surface vertex closest to the scalp used lies << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_scalp_min_mm >> mm below it (sections not shown). Whether the misfit reflects a displaced surface or the weaker contrast of an average image was not settled. No template surface was repaired, and an offset of that size was not propagated to the template's results.

The head circumference on the MRI boundary minus that on the scalp used was << F.rev_qc_children_ofc_mri_minus_surface_mm_range >> mm in the children (<< F.rev_qc_children_ofc_mri_cm_range >> against << F.rev_qc_children_ofc_surface_cm_range >> cm) and << F.rev_qc_adult_ofc_mri_minus_surface_mm >> mm in the adult (<< F.rev_qc_adult_ofc_mri_cm >> against << F.rev_qc_adult_ofc_surface_cm >> cm). Measured from the MRI boundary instead of the scalp used, the median target depth changed by << F.rev_qc_children_targets_depth_change_p50_mm_range >> mm in the children, << F.rev_qc_adult_targets_depth_change_p50_mm >> mm in the adult and << F.wr_qc_templates_targets_depth_change_p50_mm_range >> mm in the templates, whose scalps lie outside their boundaries. The children's medians thus shifted as the adult's did, but their shallowest targets did not, as follows.

The shallow cortex is not an artifact of scalp placement or registration. The closest white-surface vertex lay << F.rev_qc_child_a_white_scalp_min_mm >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_white_scalp_min_mm >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_white_scalp_min_mm >> mm below the scalp used in children A, B and C (<< F.rev_qc_children_white_mri_min_mm_range >> mm below the MRI boundary), against << F.rev_qc_adult_white_scalp_min_mm >> mm in the adult and << F.rev_qc_infant2yr_white_scalp_min_mm >> mm in the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template (Fig.~\ref{fig:S6}A). The 5th percentile of the white surface's depth was << F.rev_qc_children_white_scalp_p5_mm_range >> mm in the children, against << F.rev_qc_adult_white_scalp_p5_mm >> mm in the adult and << F.rev_qc_infant2yr_white_scalp_p5_mm >> mm in that template. This is a comparison with the adult; no pediatric norm is applied. In no child does the T1 head mask reach a face of the volume other than the inferior one. It reaches that face at the neck in children B and C, as in the adult, so the field of view does not cut the head above the neck.

Within << F.wr_qc_near_lo_mm >> mm of the scalp used lay << F.rev_qc_child_a_white_scalp_lt8mm_area_cm2 >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_white_scalp_lt8mm_area_cm2 >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_white_scalp_lt8mm_area_cm2 >> cm² of white surface in children A, B and C, and within << F.wr_qc_near_lo_mm >> mm of the MRI boundary << F.rev_qc_child_a_white_mri_lt8mm_area_cm2 >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_white_mri_lt8mm_area_cm2 >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_white_mri_lt8mm_area_cm2 >> cm² (adult: none; Fig.~\ref{fig:S6}B). Of the cortical targets, << F.rev_qc_child_a_targets_lt8mm_n >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_targets_lt8mm_n >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_targets_lt8mm_n >> lay below << F.wr_qc_near_lo_mm >> mm, and << F.rev_qc_child_a_targets_lt10mm_n >>, << F.g3b_child_b_shallow_targets_lt10mm >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_targets_lt10mm_n >> below << F.wr_qc_near_hi_mm >> mm (adult: << F.rev_qc_adult_targets_lt10mm_n >>; counted from the full-precision depths). Measured to the MRI head boundary instead of the scalp used, child B's targets less than 10 mm deep fell from << F.g3b_child_b_shallow_targets_lt10mm >> to << F.wr_qc_child_b_targets_lt10mm_mri_n >>. The verdicts were child A << F.rev_qc_child_a_verdict >>, child B << F.rev_qc_child_b_verdict >> and child C << F.rev_qc_child_c_verdict >> (<< F.rev_qc_n_children_usable >> of << F.g3b_n_children_heads >> usable). No surface was repaired, and no analysis needed rerunning.

The children's shallow cortex, and child B's small head around a near-adult cortex, are therefore not explained by their scalp surfaces or registration. One explanation the data leave open is a head boundary that the T1 images themselves place too deep. Such a boundary would bring the FreeSurfer scalp, which follows the image's edge, toward the cortex together with the dataset's watershed surfaces (inner skull a median << F.g3b_children_watershed_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >> mm below the scalp; Section~\ref{sec:S4.1}), and a check against the same images cannot detect it. An independent measurement of the scalp and skull over the upper head, from another sequence or modality, would test it.

The check cannot settle whether the white surface is anatomically correct within a few millimeters of the scalp, where the modeled scalp-and-skull layer is thinner than the adult's, nor whether three children of one dataset represent their age. The verdicts test registration and the scalp's position against the T1. They do not test anatomical plausibility, such as tissue thicknesses against norms or head circumference against centiles for age.

The source rule (no source within 4 mm of the inner skull) left out << F.wr_excl_child_a_pct >>, << F.wr_excl_child_b_pct >> and << F.wr_excl_child_c_pct >> of the white-surface area of children A, B and C, against << F.wr_excl_adult_pct >> in the adult. The adult's share is the << F.rev_ns_near_area_pct >> of Section~\ref{sec:S3.6} plus the little cortex outside the inner skull, << F.wr_excl_adult_lt10mm_cm2 >> cm² of it within 10 mm of the scalp. The rule left out << F.wr_excl_scaled_pct_range >> in the scaled adults and << F.wr_excl_templates_pct_range >> in the templates. In the children, the excluded area includes << F.wr_excl_children_lt8mm_pct_range >> of the cortex within 8 mm of the scalp: of child B's << F.wr_excl_child_b_lt8mm_n >> white-surface vertices there, << F.wr_excl_child_b_lt8mm_usable_n >> pass the source rule. For these reasons, the main text treats the children's results as provisional geometric examples.

